# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.js
.yarn/install-state.gz

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# local env files
.env
.env*.local
.env.dsql

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

.vercel

# Rendered demo video drafts (uploaded to YouTube, not tracked)
demo-out/*.mp4
# AWS console screenshots (uploaded to Devpost gallery, not tracked — contain account ID / personal email)
demo-out/*.jpg
demo-out/*.jpeg

# Scratch probe scripts. These are throwaway files written at the repo root to
# exercise live behaviour; one of them once carried a TOTP secret into a commit.
_*.ts

# Throwaway QA fixtures. They label themselves "not committed" and three of
# them still reached the repo, because `git add -A` plus a `git checkout -- .`
# that restored deletions is enough to defeat any convention.
scripts/scratch-*.ts

# Design reference renders at the repo root (relay1.png … relay4.png). Source
# material for the visual system in public/assets, kept locally and deliberately
# never committed: 19 MB of AI renders in a PUBLIC repo, to produce a set of
# SVGs that together weigh 49 KB. Anchored to the root so a legitimately-needed
# relay*.png under public/ or docs/ still tracks normally.
/relay[0-9]*.png

# Screenshots written by scripts/e2e-ui.ts. Run artefacts, not source.
e2e-*.png

# Phase 0 laptop credential, written by scripts/scratch-phase0.mjs. Never commit.
.relay-dev-key.json

# Sysadmin profile pointer for migrations. Holds NO secrets (AWS_PROFILE only),
# but never commit it regardless.
.env.admin

# The read-only verification identity (IAM relay-ro -> DB role relay_ro,
# migration 039). Listed BEFORE the file was ever created, because this
# repository is PUBLIC and a credential committed here is world-readable.
# It grants dsql:DbConnect and NOTHING else -- no KMS -- so it can read
# metadata and can never decrypt a vault. It is still production PII.
.env.ro

# Beta cohort input and the issued claim codes. REAL PEOPLE and LIVE CREDENTIALS —
# the codes are readable exactly once at issue, so .relay-cohort-codes.json is the
# only copy. Never commit either. See scripts/invite-cohort.ts.
.relay-cohort.json
.relay-cohort-codes.json

# The submitter's own contact details, required with every editorial submission
# (caregiver.com: "Include name address and current telephone number and email
# address with all submissions"). A REAL HOME ADDRESS AND PHONE NUMBER in a
# PUBLIC repository, permanent in history the moment it lands — so it lives here
# for the same reason .relay-cohort.json does, and docs reference the path, never
# the values. Written by hand; see docs/oped-angle-3-draft.md's send checklist.
.relay-submitter.json

# The autonomous-sprint mutex, written in the MAIN checkout while a sprint holds
# it and deleted on every exit path. It carries a pid, so committing one would
# outlive the process that took it and make the next sprint refuse to start for a
# process that died days ago. ~/.claude/commands/sprint.md §1.0 requires this line
# to exist before the lock is ever written.
.claude/sprint.lock
.e1-scratch/
.drill-scratch/

# B12.i — the off-GitHub heartbeat writes a stamp every few minutes.
# Local operator state, never committed (same treatment as .drill-scratch/).
.heartbeat/
